    "title": "Jackson, Summit Twp. fire department collaboration begins - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Sean Graney ,\n6 hours ago\nJACKSON, Mich. (WLNS) — Jackson and Summit Township fire crews are locking in a new era of joint service collaboration.\nFire officials say the agreement passed on Nov. 19 sets the stage to rethink how both departments respond to emergencies.\n(WLNS) Jackson Fire Chief Tim Gonzales says the collaboration is about building a smarter, more sustainable, service model for Jackson County.\n“The previous responses that we’ve had, we’ve kind of exhausted everything that we could do at that lower level,” Gonzales said. “So, what this has allows us to do is to think outside of the box more and create a longer lasting sustainable model for the fire service in Jackson County.”\n(WLNS) Gonzales says they have pushed their current mutual aid system as far as it can go, and this new agreement will prioritize bigger and longer lasting solutions.\n“We’re going to use data to identify the proper amount of apparatus and also create an apparatus replacement program for both departments,” Gonzales said.\n(WLNS) Summit Township Supervisor John Griffin says one of the largest advantages is flexibility and sharing staffing when either side needs help — which he says an also save money.\n“If we can borrow someone from the city of Jackson for a shift, they can do the same for us. We can cover it. We can reduce overtime and that’s a big cost. That overtime is a big cost,” Griffin said.\nSummit Township Fire Chief Tim McEldowney says safety remains the top priority and teaming up with the city means more reliable and safer responses for all.\n(WLNS) “Also, for (the) safety of our firefighters, making sure that we have adequate staff for every type of incident,” McEldowney said.\nGriffin says they’ll evaluate the partnership after a year of services in order to determine the best path forward for public safety.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WLNS 6 News.",
